{{Politics of Balisca}}

'''Baliscan nationality law''' ({{Langx|bl|Ley de Nacionalidad Baliscana}}) governs the principles of citizenship and nationality within the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. It is a legal framework that is uniquely shaped by Balisca's history of [[Immigration to Balisca|immigration]], its foundational commitment to a "[[W:Plurinationalism|plurinational state]]", and its [[Politics of Balisca|libertarian socialist]] ideology. The law is distinguished by its relatively inclusive approach, which combines universal birthright citizenship with a straightforward path to naturalization and an active role for citizens in the confederation's governance.

== Acquisition of citizenship ==

=== Birth (''[[W:Jus soli|jus soli]]'') ===
Baliscan nationality law is founded on the principle of [[W:Jus soli|''jus soli'']], or "birthright citizenship". Since the progressive reforms of the [[Anyos Federales|Reconstruction era]], every child born on Baliscan soil is a Baliscan national, regardless of the nationality or origin of their parents. This universal birthright citizenship was a foundational measure designed to eliminate the class and [[W:Caste systems|caste systems]] that the confederation inherited from its colonial past and to ensure that all individuals born into the Confederation are considered equal citizens.

=== Descent (''[[W:Jus sanguinis|jus sanguinis]]'') ===
The principle of [[W:Jus sanguinis|''jus sanguinis'']], or citizenship by descent, was expanded in the 1880s. Under this reform, a child born to at least one Baliscan parent on foreign land is also considered a Baliscan national. This provision was implemented to support the large Baliscan diaspora and to ensure that Baliscans living and working abroad could maintain their ties to the Confederation and pass on their citizenship to their children, granting them all the benefits of Baliscan citizenship.

=== Naturalization ===
The path to naturalization is deliberately simple and inclusive. The process is open to all persons who:

* Have resided in Balisca continuously for a minimum of two years.

* Pass an oral exam on Baliscan history and values (e.g., [[W:Horizontalidad|horizontalidad]], [[Convivencia (Baliscan context)|Convivencia]], [[Consensual confederation|consensual democracy]]).

* Express fluent knowledge of [[Baliscano language|Baliscano]] or one of the territorialized languages ([[Volisanian language|Volisanian]], [[Xacano language|Xacano]], [[Ajami]], etc).

Dual nationality is recognized, although depending on the individual's country of origin, naturalization may require applicants to renounce their previous nationalities (such as in the case of [[Potocsí|Potocsí citizens]]) in lieu of stricter residency requirements. However, persons convicted of certain criminal acts (such as [[W:Hate crime|hate crimes]]) or offenses against the state may be permanently barred from naturalization. The requisite period of residence may be subject to certain conditions or exceptions, such as completion of integration courses or marital status to Baliscan citizens. Additionally, citizens of specific countries or regions may be exempt from certain requirements or granted special considerations during the naturalization process. The number of individuals naturalized as Baliscan citizens has been consistently increasing since the end of the [[Great Adonian War]].

